course: dictionary
lesson: 11222
title: Woodpecker, the bird that hammers
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A woodpecker is a bird that clings to a tree trunk and hammers into the wood with its strong, sharp beak. It does this to find insects hiding under the bark and to dig a hole for its nest. «Real woodpeckers drill holes to nest and to find insects, and drum on wood to call.» The words holes, nest, insects and wood are the clues. Try your meaning in the sentence: woodpeckers hammer holes in trees to nest, and that still makes sense.
explain: Sometimes an author uses a word in a funny way. «Rory, pressing buttons like a happy woodpecker, bought a pack of gems.» Rory is not a bird. The sentence means he was tapping the buttons quickly and busily, like a bird hammering on a tree. Ask yourself what the picture in the word adds to the sentence.
explain: Read the whole sentence before you decide. «The noctule roosts in old woodpecker holes» tells you woodpecker holes are holes that woodpeckers have made, and that other animals can move into them later.
fact: A woodpecker is a noun: one woodpecker, many woodpeckers.
fact: A woodpecker is a bird that clings to tree trunks and hammers into the wood with its beak.
fact: Woodpeckers use their long, sticky tongue to pull out grubs from inside the wood.
fact: Their skull is thick, and it is built to soften the blows from all that hammering.
fact: In Britain you may see the great spotted woodpecker and the green woodpecker.
exercise: «Real woodpeckers drill holes to nest and to find insects.» What is a woodpecker? | A bat that flies at night | *A bird that hammers into trees with its beak | A fish that swims in rivers | A tree that grows in woods
exercise: «Rory, pressing buttons like a happy woodpecker, bought a pack of gems.» What does woodpecker suggest here? | *Quick, busy tapping | Slow and sleepy movements | Loud singing | Flying high over the trees
exercise: Which of these is a woodpecker you may see in Britain? | Elf owl | Sparrow | *Great spotted woodpecker | Noctule bat
test: What does a woodpecker hit trees with to make its holes? | *Its strong, sharp beak | Its soft tail | Its long legs | Its wings
test: Why is a woodpecker's skull thick? | To keep its tongue warm | To help it fly faster | *To soften the blows from its hammering | To hide it from owls
exam: Which word means a bird that hammers into tree trunks with its beak? | owl | sparrow | noctule | *woodpecker
